---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro'
import { pricing, formatPrice } from '@/data/pricing'
import { site } from '@/data/site'
---

<Base
  title="Notch terms"
  description="Plain-language terms for buying and using the Notch Windows app."
>
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="page-head">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Terms</span>
      <h1>Terms, in plain language.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Short version: one payment, a license that does not expire, a 14-day refund if it is not
        for you.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What you buy</h2>
      <p>
        Notch is a paid Windows app sold as a one-time license — not a subscription. The price is
        {formatPrice(pricing.earlyBird.price)} for the first {pricing.earlyBird.seats} licenses
        (early bird) and {formatPrice(pricing.standard.price)} afterwards. Early buyers keep their
        price permanently; the later price never applies to them. Every license includes all
        features and all Notch v1 updates.
      </p>

      <h2>Payment</h2>
      <p>
        Checkout is handled by a third-party payment provider. We never see or store your card
        details. Prices are in USD. See the
        <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a> for what happens to purchase-related data.
      </p>

      <h2>Refunds</h2>
      <p>
        If Notch is not for you, email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> within
        14 days of purchase for a full refund, no questions asked.
      </p>

      <h2>What the license allows</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Use Notch on your own Windows PCs, personally or at work.</li>
        <li>Do not resell, redistribute or repackage the app or your license.</li>
        <li>The source is public at <a href={site.repoUrl} rel="noopener">the repository</a>; check the license there before redistributing code.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Third-party products</h2>
      <p>
        Notch integrates with third-party products such as Spotify and local AI coding tools, but
        is not affiliated with or endorsed by them. Those products have their own terms that still
        apply when you use them together with Notch.
      </p>

      <h2>Posture</h2>
      <p>
        Do not use the posture feature as a medical device or a substitute for professional advice.
        It is an awareness aid, not a measurement instrument.
      </p>

      <h2>No warranty</h2>
      <p>
        The build is unsigned and provided as-is, without warranty. Windows may show a standard
        “unknown publisher” warning; every release is produced by a public workflow with a
        published SHA-256 checksum.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        These terms may change with a release. Continued use means you accept the current version.
        The price you paid is never affected — existing licenses keep their purchase price.
      </p>

      <p>
        Contact: <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
